    "body": "AI startup Evos raised $9 million in pre-seed funding to develop autonomous software operators for small and midsized industrial businesses, including freight brokerages, the company announced Wednesday.\nThis comes as the company looks to automate routine work without requiring businesses to replace their existing software.\nThe round was co-led by Uncork Capital and Field Ventures, with participation from Crosslink Capital, Utopia Capital Management, Sandwith Ventures and angel investors.\nEvos said it surpassed $1 million in annual recurring revenue four weeks after launching. The company did not disclose its valuation or the number of customers using its platform.\nHow it works\nEvos is developing AI operators that learn how employees make decisions and carry out specific business functions within a company’s existing systems. Rather than requiring employees to prompt an AI tool for every task, the operators are designed to manage an assigned function independently once their decisions consistently match those of experienced workers.\nThe approach targets smaller industrial businesses where operational knowledge often rests with experienced employees rather than documented procedures. Evos said its operators learn from employees’ behavior and decisions, with the goal of reproducing their work before taking on tasks autonomously.\n“The most capable system in many of these businesses is the person who has been there 20 years, and what they know has never been written down,” said Urav Shah, founder and CEO of Evos.\nAccording to the company’s announcement, 99.3% of U.S. motor carriers operate fewer than 100 trucks, while 93% of U.S. manufacturers have fewer than 100 employees. Evos argues that these businesses need automation that does not require extensive technical resources or lengthy implementation projects.\n“The platform is fully self-serve: work that would take a consultant or an implementation partner 9 to 18 months to deliver, an Evos operator takes on in 24 hours from discovery to deployment,” the company said.\nOne freight brokerage, MyFreightWorld, is using an Evos operator to audit less-than-truckload (LTL) carrier invoices against its transportation management system . Evos said the operator made nearly 3,500 audit decisions over two months with 97% accuracy and identified billing discrepancies valued at more than $600,000 over the most recent five-week period. The $600,000 represents the value of discrepancies identified between invoices involving MyFreightWorld and its end customers, rather than money recovered or savings realized, Evos noted. The company did not explain how it calculated the 97% accuracy rate or specify the sample used to measure performance.\nBroader context\nLTL invoice audits can uncover billing errors involving accessorial fees, fuel surcharges and freight classifications.\nA 2024 analysis by AFS Logistics illustrates the volume of discrepancies that can arise in LTL billing. The company found 11,881 errors in a review of nearly 250,000 invoices. Inaccurate accessorial charges accounted for 42.8% of the errors, followed by fuel surcharges at 14.4% and freight misclassification at 12.3%. The findings reflect AFS’s review rather than the entire LTL market.\nThe startup’s approach comes as logistics companies continue to seek measurable returns from AI investments. A Boston Consulting Group report published in August 2026 found that only 13% of executives surveyed at 30 leading global logistics companies said AI was delivering measurable financial results, despite two-thirds of respondents reporting a dedicated AI budget and strategy. The findings highlight the gap between investing in AI and translating that investment into measurable business results.\nFor smaller operators, the cost and complexity of implementation can also determine whether automation is practical. Evos is betting that software capable of learning established workflows and executing tasks within existing systems can lower those barriers.\nWhy this matters\nAI startups are increasingly targeting routine logistics work, but proving that automation delivers measurable financial results remains a challenge. Evos’ work with MyFreightWorld offers a specific example of how AI operators could be used in freight brokerage operations, though the startup has not disclosed how much money the technology has recovered or independently verified its reported accuracy rate.\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
